<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis Philippe I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1773">1773</a>–<a href="/years/1850">1850</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Louis-Philippe of Orléans, Louis Philippe, Duke of Orléans, Louis-Philippe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis Philippe I was King of the French from 1830 to 1848. Head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> and son of a prince who had backed the Revolution, he was placed on the throne after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/july-revolution">July Revolution</a> of 1830 as a constitutional 'citizen king'. His July Monarchy favoured the wealthy middle class, and he was overthrown in the Revolution of 1848 and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i.jpg" alt="Louis Philippe I" width="320" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Xaver Winterhalter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1841_portrait_painting_of_Louis_Philippe_I_(King_of_the_French)_by_Winterhalter.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Louis Philippe was born on 6 October 1773 in the Palais-Royal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, the eldest son of Louis Philippe II, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, later known as Philippe Égalité. The Orléans cadet branch was distrusted by the elder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> branch, and his father was exiled from court. From 1782 the Countess of Genlis tutored him and gave him a fondness for liberal thought. He joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobin-club">Jacobin Club</a>, as his father supported.</p><p>As Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a> he commanded troops in the Revolutionary Wars and was a lieutenant general by the age of nineteen. After he was drawn into the treason of Dumouriez he fled in 1793, and his father was executed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reign-of-terror">Reign of Terror</a>. He lived under an assumed name in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and then settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. His exile lasted 21 years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s abdication Louis Philippe returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> during the Bourbon Restoration, and his resentment at the treatment of his family led him to side with the liberal opposition. In 1830 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/july-revolution">July Revolution</a> overthrew Charles X, who abdicated in favour of his grandson Henri and named Louis Philippe Lieutenant général du royaume. Louis Philippe did not announce the wish that Henri should succeed, and the Chamber of Deputies, aware of his liberal record and popularity, proclaimed him king.</p><p>He ruled in an unpretentious style, but his support came from the wealthy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a>. He was called the Citizen King and was at first much loved, but his government came to seem increasingly conservative. From 1840 to 1848 he followed conservative policies under the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-guizot">François Guizot</a>. He promoted friendship with the United Kingdom and colonial expansion, notably the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, and he survived seven assassination attempts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Economic conditions deteriorated in 1847. On 24 February 1848, during the Revolution, Louis Philippe abdicated in favour of his nine-year-old grandson Philippe and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in disguise. He fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on a boat offered by the British consul at Le Havre. The Second Republic was proclaimed on 26 February. Louis Philippe lived in exile in the United Kingdom until his death on 26 August 1850. His supporters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> are known as Orléanists, and his grandchildren included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-of-belgium">Leopold II of Belgium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlota-of-mexico">Empress Carlota</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis Philippe I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 9 September 1842, under threat of bombardment, Pōmare IV and her leading chiefs signed a request for French protection, which <b>Louis-Philippe</b> ratified in March 1843.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-tahiti">Kingdom of Tahiti · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After protests in Paris on 22 February and the army's massacre of 65 demonstrators on 23 February, <b>Louis Philippe I</b> abdicated on 24 February.</blockquote><a href="/movement/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848 · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis XVIII granted France a Charter in 1814, and the July Monarchy of 1830 made <b>Louis Philippe I</b> “King of the French” rather than “King of France”.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Louis Philippe I</b> began the Museum of the History of France in 1833, inaugurated on 30 June 1837 and dedicated to &quot;all the glories of France&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/work/palace-of-versailles">Palace of Versailles · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Commissioned by Napoleon in 1806 to honour his armies, the Arc de Triomphe was finally inaugurated under King <b>Louis-Philippe</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-29">29 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Three days of revolt in Paris drove him into exile, and <b>Louis-Philippe of Orléans</b> became king, beginning the July Monarchy.</blockquote><a href="/state/bourbon-restoration">Bourbon Restoration</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Claremont, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1823" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Louis Philippe I"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5132 5945h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5132 5945h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1823">What was here in 1823? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
